<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhumihar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhumihars are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> landholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> status. Many held large zamindari estates under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the British. The Rajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> were a Bhumihar family, as were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of Hathwa and Bettiah, and Bhumihars were prominent in the politics of 20th-century Bihar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhumihar.jpg" alt="Bhumihar" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BOURNE &amp;amp; SHEPHERD · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Majaraja_of_Benares_and_Suite,_1870s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
